<h1>Files cross as descriptors</h1>
<p>The current release moves asset payloads out of broker JSON frames and adds bounded streaming between provider handles and native HTTP.</p>
</header>
<p class="release-receipt"><strong>{{ release.tag }}</strong> · {{ release.date }} · <a href="{{ release.url }}">Release notes ↗</a> · <a href="{{ release.sourceUrl }}">{{ release.shortRevision }}</a>
</p>
<section id="changes"><span class="compat-anchor" id="release-summary-title" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<h2>Three changes to file handling</h2>
<div class="guide-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Change comparison">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col">Change</th>
<th scope="col">Practical consequence</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Descriptor transport</th>
<td data-label="Practical consequence">The gateway passes read-only descriptors over the local Unix socket. Wasm receives handles; bounded guest reads still copy bytes.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Streamed HTTP bodies</th>
<td data-label="Practical consequence">Native HTTP encodes and hashes exact-length uploads. Each asset occurrence counts toward decoded-content ceilings.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Separate disk volume</th>
<td data-label="Practical consequence">Chart {{ release.chart.publishedVersion }} provisions a broker-only 320Mi asset volume; custom broker config must name it explicitly.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="migration"><span class="compat-anchor" id="release-details-title" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="compat-anchor" id="release-path-title" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<h2>Upgrade core, providers and configuration together.</h2>
<p>Rebuild asset-producing providers for the current <code>dekopon:asset</code> interfaces before relying on file workflows. Old base64 attachment result envelopes are refused. <code>providerAttachments</code> and <code>chatAssetInputs</code> no longer parse, even when empty.</p>
<p>References resolve automatically, but delivery requires an <code>asset.send</code> grant. Attach retains a file; it does not send it. <a href="/guides/runtime/#assets">Files and delivery limits</a>.</p>
<p><code>maxHttpRequestBytes</code> covers request metadata and literal body parts; assets have decoded-content accounting. Disk retention counts stored bytes instead. <a href="/deploy/kubernetes/#state">Configure the asset volume and broker path</a>.</p>
</section>
<section id="limits"><span class="compat-anchor" id="release-scope-title" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<h2>What this release does not change</h2>
<p>Model image requests still carry image data; this is not end-to-end zero-copy transport. Asset references and queued sends do not survive restart. Check independently versioned providers for compatible asset support rather than treating a core release as their publication.</p>
<p>Telemetry payload attributes stop at 4096 bytes and retain full-length byte counts. Model request bodies use compact encoding with Content-Length; the model leg still allocates image data. <a href="/guides/traces/#privacy">Telemetry visibility</a>.</p>
<p>The release’s image-memory experiment used a measurement-only provider build, not the published gpt-image component. It is not a reproducible shipping-workflow benchmark or a per-request memory guarantee.</p>
</section>
